1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,348
Computational Skepticism, Exam Prep.

2
00:00:01,398 --> 00:00:02,397
Key Terms: Robustness.

3
00:00:02,447 --> 00:00:07,290
Thirty-four terms for asking what a model
actually learned, and what a small change

4
00:00:07,340 --> 00:00:07,639
reveals.

5
00:00:07,689 --> 00:00:13,231
You will be able to name each attack and
each defense, and say exactly what a

6
00:00:13,281 --> 00:00:14,280
robustness claim covers.

7
00:00:15,430 --> 00:00:15,647
Hallo.

8
00:00:15,697 --> 00:00:17,250
This is Liam, in for Bear.

9
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:21,525
A tiny change flips a model's answer, and
people say the model is fragile, so harden

10
00:00:21,575 --> 00:00:21,792
it.

11
00:00:21,842 --> 00:00:25,800
The better reading is that the model
learned a proxy, a signal that went with

12
00:00:25,850 --> 00:00:27,937
the right label without being the thing
itself.

13
00:00:27,987 --> 00:00:31,410
This film goes through thirty-four terms
for that, each one shown on screen.

14
00:00:31,760 --> 00:00:34,662
Five labels sit in the corners of the
pictures that follow.

15
00:00:34,712 --> 00:00:37,882
In the book text: the course text uses and
explains the term.

16
00:00:37,932 --> 00:00:42,443
In the glossary: the term is on the course
term list, but the text does not explain

17
00:00:42,493 --> 00:00:42,712
it.

18
00:00:42,762 --> 00:00:47,005
Simulation: a made-up run with a fixed
seed, so it comes out the same every time.

19
00:00:47,055 --> 00:00:49,688
Illustrative data: numbers chosen to show
the idea, not measured.

20
00:00:49,738 --> 00:00:52,640
Conceptual: a drawing of the idea, with no
data behind it.

21
00:00:52,990 --> 00:00:56,913
This is a map of thirty-four terms in four
groups.

22
00:00:56,963 --> 00:01:01,284
The first group asks why a small change
flips the answer.

23
00:01:01,334 --> 00:01:04,860
It holds adversarial example, proxy,
robust features, non-robust features,

24
00:01:04,910 --> 00:01:08,833
FGSM, the Fast Gradient Sign Method,
perturbation budget, linearity hypothesis,

25
00:01:08,883 --> 00:01:10,820
low-variance direction and boundary
tilting.

26
00:01:11,170 --> 00:01:13,525
The second group is the ways in.

27
00:01:13,575 --> 00:01:16,616
White-box attack, black-box attack,
transfer attack, patch attack, natural

28
00:01:16,666 --> 00:01:17,990
adversarial and prompt injection.

29
00:01:18,040 --> 00:01:23,830
Each one is defined by what the attacker
has, and by what it exploits in the model.

30
00:01:24,180 --> 00:01:27,292
The third group is defenses and their
limits.

31
00:01:27,342 --> 00:01:30,454
PGD, Projected Gradient Descent,
adversarial training, robustness trade-

32
00:01:30,504 --> 00:01:33,616
off, randomized smoothing, Lipschitz
constant, formal verification, detection,

33
00:01:33,666 --> 00:01:35,197
preprocessing and adaptive attack.

34
00:01:35,247 --> 00:01:39,940
For each tool, know what it buys and what
it cannot do.

35
00:01:40,290 --> 00:01:43,838
The last group is saying what you know.

36
00:01:43,888 --> 00:01:47,435
Distribution shift, semantic invariance,
prompt sensitivity, adversarial testing,

37
00:01:47,485 --> 00:01:51,033
robustness profile, residual risk,
monitoring, Pearl's ladder, counterfactual

38
00:01:51,083 --> 00:01:52,382
and governance question.

39
00:01:52,432 --> 00:01:55,980
These are the terms for reporting
robustness honestly.

40
00:01:56,330 --> 00:01:58,299
One input, before and after a push.

41
00:01:58,349 --> 00:02:02,626
An adversarial example is an input changed
on purpose, too slightly for a person to

42
00:02:02,676 --> 00:02:04,068
notice, so the answer flips.

43
00:02:04,118 --> 00:02:08,683
In this simulated run no value moves more
than zero point two, and the model says

44
00:02:08,733 --> 00:02:08,972
gibbon.

45
00:02:09,022 --> 00:02:12,433
For example, an applicant slips a few
invisible characters into a résumé.

46
00:02:12,483 --> 00:02:16,472
A recruiter sees the same page, but the
screening model flips from reject to

47
00:02:16,522 --> 00:02:16,760
interview.

48
00:02:17,110 --> 00:02:18,143
Now the original input.

49
00:02:18,193 --> 00:02:20,849
The red texture bar carries ninety-three
percent of the score.

50
00:02:20,899 --> 00:02:24,910
Texture is a proxy, a signal that tracked
the label in training without being the

51
00:02:24,960 --> 00:02:25,451
thing itself.

52
00:02:25,501 --> 00:02:27,346
So inspect what the model relies on.

53
00:02:27,396 --> 00:02:28,699
Do not just patch it.

54
00:02:28,749 --> 00:02:32,759
For example, a résumé screener trained on
past hires leans on a few buzzwords those

55
00:02:32,809 --> 00:02:33,842
hires happened to use.

56
00:02:33,892 --> 00:02:37,090
The words went with good hires, but they
are not the skill.

57
00:02:37,440 --> 00:02:38,549
Two kinds of feature.

58
00:02:38,599 --> 00:02:42,896
Shape is one of the robust features, tied
to the label and hard to move.

59
00:02:42,946 --> 00:02:46,663
A push of zero point two is ten percent of
the class gap.

60
00:02:46,713 --> 00:02:48,111
Texture shows the non-robust features.

61
00:02:48,161 --> 00:02:52,458
It predicts the label too, but the same
push is sixty-seven percent of the gap.

62
00:02:52,508 --> 00:02:56,225
For example, in a résumé screener, the
projects someone actually built are robust

63
00:02:56,275 --> 00:02:56,515
features.

64
00:02:56,565 --> 00:02:59,123
The exact wording of the skills line is
non-robust.

65
00:02:59,173 --> 00:03:02,600
It predicted hires in training, and a
single swapped word moves it.

66
00:03:02,950 --> 00:03:05,962
A table of weights, and the step each
input gets.

67
00:03:06,012 --> 00:03:10,555
FGSM, the Fast Gradient Sign Method, takes
one step of size epsilon on every input,

68
00:03:10,605 --> 00:03:12,393
along the sign of the gradient.

69
00:03:12,443 --> 00:03:16,986
Every step helps the attacker, so in this
simulated run they total zero point four

70
00:03:17,036 --> 00:03:17,292
seven.

71
00:03:17,342 --> 00:03:21,273
For example, picture a model that predicts
interviews from numeric fields such as

72
00:03:21,323 --> 00:03:22,192
years of experience.

73
00:03:22,242 --> 00:03:27,091
FGSM nudges every field by the same small
amount, up or down as the gradient says,

74
00:03:27,141 --> 00:03:28,010
in one step.

75
00:03:28,360 --> 00:03:30,233
Three ways to spend the same budget.

76
00:03:30,283 --> 00:03:33,805
The perturbation budget, epsilon, is the
most any single input value may change,

77
00:03:33,855 --> 00:03:35,454
also called the L infinity budget.

78
00:03:35,504 --> 00:03:37,377
It caps each value, not the total.

79
00:03:37,427 --> 00:03:41,224
The red bar follows the sign of every
weight and spends all of it.

80
00:03:41,274 --> 00:03:45,070
For example, someone auditing a résumé
screener lets each field, such as a test

81
00:03:45,120 --> 00:03:47,543
score, move by at most a small fixed
amount.

82
00:03:47,593 --> 00:03:51,390
Every field may use the full amount, so
the total change can be large.

83
00:03:51,740 --> 00:03:55,330
The same tiny step, zero point zero one,
on every input value.

84
00:03:55,380 --> 00:03:59,577
The linearity hypothesis says such pushes
add up when the score is locally linear.

85
00:03:59,627 --> 00:04:01,093
Ten inputs barely move it.

86
00:04:01,143 --> 00:04:05,340
About one hundred fifty thousand inputs
move it by fifty-nine point nine, past the

87
00:04:05,390 --> 00:04:05,947
red tick.

88
00:04:05,997 --> 00:04:10,497
Picture a screener that reads a scanned
résumé as an image of many thousands of

89
00:04:10,547 --> 00:04:10,800
pixels.

90
00:04:10,850 --> 00:04:15,047
Shading each pixel by an invisible amount
barely moves the score, but summed over

91
00:04:15,097 --> 00:04:17,170
every pixel it can flip the call.

92
00:04:17,520 --> 00:04:22,333
Two groups of points, wide from side to
side and thin up and down, with boundary A

93
00:04:22,383 --> 00:04:22,905
between them.

94
00:04:22,955 --> 00:04:26,910
A low-variance direction is one where the
training data barely varies, here up and

95
00:04:26,960 --> 00:04:27,196
down.

96
00:04:27,246 --> 00:04:28,912
Training said almost nothing about it.

97
00:04:28,962 --> 00:04:32,917
For example, every résumé in a screener's
training set used almost the same font

98
00:04:32,967 --> 00:04:33,203
size.

99
00:04:33,253 --> 00:04:36,922
Font size barely varied, so training
taught the model almost nothing about what

100
00:04:36,972 --> 00:04:37,780
it should mean.

101
00:04:38,130 --> 00:04:42,243
Boundary tilting means a boundary that
runs almost alongside the data, like the

102
00:04:42,293 --> 00:04:43,524
red line, boundary B.

103
00:04:43,574 --> 00:04:48,008
In this simulated run both boundaries get
all sixty points right, but the nearest

104
00:04:48,058 --> 00:04:53,132
point is zero point eight from boundary A
and zero point zero seven from boundary B.

105
00:04:53,182 --> 00:04:56,975
For example, a screener's boundary sorts
every past résumé correctly, yet runs

106
00:04:57,025 --> 00:05:00,177
almost alongside them, a hair away in the
font-size direction.

107
00:05:00,227 --> 00:05:03,700
A résumé in slightly larger type lands on
the other side.

108
00:05:04,050 --> 00:05:08,473
The ringed point steps zero point zero
nine in the quiet, low-variance direction

109
00:05:08,523 --> 00:05:09,849
and crosses boundary B.

110
00:05:09,899 --> 00:05:11,569
Boundary A does not change.

111
00:05:11,619 --> 00:05:12,601
Keep these apart.

112
00:05:12,651 --> 00:05:15,354
The decision boundary is where the answer
changes.

113
00:05:15,404 --> 00:05:18,450
The data manifold is where the training
data sits.

114
00:05:18,800 --> 00:05:21,819
A chart of how many correct predictions
each attack flips.

115
00:05:21,869 --> 00:05:25,194
In a white-box attack, also called
gradient-based, the attacker has the

116
00:05:25,244 --> 00:05:28,877
model's weights and gradients, so the best
direction comes straight from them.

117
00:05:28,927 --> 00:05:31,945
In this simulated run it flips eighty-five
point four percent.

118
00:05:31,995 --> 00:05:35,628
For example, someone gets a copy of a
company's résumé screener, weights

119
00:05:35,678 --> 00:05:39,310
included, and reads off it exactly which
words raise the score most.

120
00:05:39,660 --> 00:05:42,471
A black-box attack, also called query-
based, has no weights.

121
00:05:42,521 --> 00:05:45,903
The attacker sees only outputs and
estimates the gradient by probing, nudging

122
00:05:45,953 --> 00:05:47,620
each input value up and down.

123
00:05:47,670 --> 00:05:51,338
Here that takes forty queries per input,
and it flips the same share.

124
00:05:51,388 --> 00:05:55,915
For example, an applicant can only upload
résumés to a job portal and see the fit

125
00:05:55,965 --> 00:05:56,773
score it returns.

126
00:05:56,823 --> 00:05:59,920
They change a line, upload again, and
watch the score move.

127
00:06:00,270 --> 00:06:02,392
A transfer attack never touches the
target.

128
00:06:02,442 --> 00:06:07,667
The push is built on a surrogate, a stand-
in model trained on similar data, and it

129
00:06:07,717 --> 00:06:09,528
still flips eighty-four point seven
percent.

130
00:06:09,578 --> 00:06:11,080
Similar data teaches similar proxies.

131
00:06:11,130 --> 00:06:12,321
Black-box queries the target.

132
00:06:12,371 --> 00:06:13,252
Transfer does not.

133
00:06:13,302 --> 00:06:17,595
For example, a job seeker trains a
practice screener on public job posts and

134
00:06:17,645 --> 00:06:21,939
résumés, finds edits that fool it, then
sends that résumé to a company screener

135
00:06:21,989 --> 00:06:22,870
they never queried.

136
00:06:23,220 --> 00:06:25,349
Two pixel grids with the same score drop.

137
00:06:25,399 --> 00:06:26,984
Left, every pixel changes a little.

138
00:06:27,034 --> 00:06:30,798
Right, a patch attack, a big change in a
small region, like a sticker.

139
00:06:30,848 --> 00:06:34,612
In this simulated run it touches six
percent of the pixels, and it survives

140
00:06:34,662 --> 00:06:34,885
printing.

141
00:06:34,935 --> 00:06:39,244
For example, an applicant prints a small,
bold block in one corner of a résumé that

142
00:06:39,294 --> 00:06:40,061
will be scanned.

143
00:06:40,111 --> 00:06:43,330
The rest of the page is untouched, yet the
screener's call flips.

144
00:06:43,680 --> 00:06:47,955
Natural adversarial means no attacker at
all, just an unusual, long-tail input that

145
00:06:48,005 --> 00:06:48,954
training hardly covered.

146
00:06:49,004 --> 00:06:52,946
Typical inputs score ninety-nine point
four percent, the long tail only fifteen

147
00:06:52,996 --> 00:06:53,612
point nine.

148
00:06:53,662 --> 00:06:57,937
The overall bar still says ninety-five
percent, so the average hides the failure.

149
00:06:57,987 --> 00:07:02,263
For example, a screener trained mostly on
standard office résumés meets a career

150
00:07:02,313 --> 00:07:04,592
changer whose résumé is all farm work.

151
00:07:04,642 --> 00:07:05,590
Nobody altered anything.

152
00:07:05,640 --> 00:07:09,250
The input is just rare, and the model gets
it wrong.

153
00:07:09,600 --> 00:07:13,272
Four lines an agent sees, one from the
user, three from a document.

154
00:07:13,322 --> 00:07:17,281
Prompt injection is an instruction hidden
in data, which the agent treats as a

155
00:07:17,331 --> 00:07:17,567
command.

156
00:07:17,617 --> 00:07:20,431
In one mixed stream it obeys the hidden
email line.

157
00:07:20,481 --> 00:07:22,721
With channels split, it obeys only the
user.

158
00:07:22,771 --> 00:07:27,016
For example, an applicant hides a line in
white text on a résumé, telling the

159
00:07:27,066 --> 00:07:29,307
recruiter's AI assistant to rank this
candidate first.

160
00:07:29,357 --> 00:07:32,170
The résumé is data, but the assistant
obeys the line.

161
00:07:32,520 --> 00:07:35,776
An invented loss surface inside the
budget, darker is worse.

162
00:07:35,826 --> 00:07:39,082
PGD, Projected Gradient Descent, takes
many small gradient-sign steps, each

163
00:07:39,132 --> 00:07:40,735
projected back inside the budget.

164
00:07:40,785 --> 00:07:44,041
One FGSM step lands on a corner and stays
right.

165
00:07:44,091 --> 00:07:47,678
Ten PGD steps reach the dark patch, and
the answer flips.

166
00:07:47,728 --> 00:07:51,976
For example, an attacker nudges an
application's numeric fields a little at a

167
00:07:52,026 --> 00:07:53,960
time, the way the gradient points.

168
00:07:54,010 --> 00:07:58,588
After each nudge, any field past the
budget is pulled back inside, until the

169
00:07:58,638 --> 00:07:59,580
interview call flips.

170
00:07:59,930 --> 00:08:04,077
Adversarial training means training on the
worst push an attacker could find, at

171
00:08:04,127 --> 00:08:04,723
every step.

172
00:08:04,773 --> 00:08:08,919
In this simulated run the standard model
falls to twenty-four point five percent

173
00:08:08,969 --> 00:08:09,565
under attack.

174
00:08:09,615 --> 00:08:12,148
The adversarially trained model holds
seventy-four point eight.

175
00:08:12,198 --> 00:08:16,667
For example, a company retrains its résumé
screener on the worst doctored résumés an

176
00:08:16,717 --> 00:08:21,187
attacker could make, found fresh at every
training step, each labeled with the right

177
00:08:21,237 --> 00:08:21,510
decision.

178
00:08:21,860 --> 00:08:25,162
Each point is a model trained against a
different budget.

179
00:08:25,212 --> 00:08:28,514
The robustness trade-off, or accuracy-
robustness trade-off, is the price.

180
00:08:28,564 --> 00:08:32,872
As the budget grows, robust accuracy
climbs, while clean accuracy, the score on

181
00:08:32,922 --> 00:08:34,213
ordinary inputs, moves left.

182
00:08:34,263 --> 00:08:37,230
Adversarial training also costs several
times more to run.

183
00:08:37,280 --> 00:08:41,923
For example, a screener trained to resist
keyword stuffing gets harder to fool, but

184
00:08:41,973 --> 00:08:44,940
it also misjudges more ordinary, honest
résumés than before.

185
00:08:45,290 --> 00:08:48,245
A grid of one thousand noisy copies of one
input.

186
00:08:48,295 --> 00:08:50,950
Randomized smoothing classifies every copy
and takes the vote.

187
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:54,856
In this simulated run nine hundred ninety-
three pick the right class, which

188
00:08:54,906 --> 00:08:57,861
certifies a radius R of zero point two
four six.

189
00:08:57,911 --> 00:09:02,068
For example, a screener scores many noisy
copies of an application, each with small

190
00:09:02,118 --> 00:09:05,374
random changes to its numeric fields, and
gives the majority call.

191
00:09:05,424 --> 00:09:08,980
A lopsided vote means no edit inside the
radius can flip it.

192
00:09:09,330 --> 00:09:11,187
More noise buys a bigger radius.

193
00:09:11,237 --> 00:09:16,592
The red bars grow from zero point one five
to zero point eight two as the noise,

194
00:09:16,642 --> 00:09:17,228
sigma, rises.

195
00:09:17,278 --> 00:09:19,135
The dark bars show the cost.

196
00:09:19,185 --> 00:09:22,950
Accuracy of the smoothed classifier falls
from one hundred percent to eighty-five.

197
00:09:23,300 --> 00:09:24,683
Output change against input change.

198
00:09:24,733 --> 00:09:28,982
The Lipschitz constant, L, caps how fast
the output can move as the input moves.

199
00:09:29,032 --> 00:09:31,274
The steep red line is the standard model.

200
00:09:31,324 --> 00:09:35,286
The flat dark line is the trained one,
whose small L bounds every push.

201
00:09:35,336 --> 00:09:39,012
For example, a salary-estimate model with
a small Lipschitz constant can only shift

202
00:09:39,062 --> 00:09:42,164
its estimate a little when the listed
experience changes a little.

203
00:09:42,214 --> 00:09:45,890
With a large one, that same small edit
could swing the estimate wildly.

204
00:09:46,240 --> 00:09:48,627
A small network's score as the budget
grows.

205
00:09:48,677 --> 00:09:52,284
Formal verification proves a property for
every input in a stated region.

206
00:09:52,334 --> 00:09:57,159
In this simulated run the grey band is
proved, up to zero point one four nine.

207
00:09:57,209 --> 00:10:00,205
A real flip sits at zero point two one
nine.

208
00:10:00,255 --> 00:10:01,119
Between them, unknown.

209
00:10:01,169 --> 00:10:05,385
For example, a team proves that, for every
application in a stated range, changing

210
00:10:05,435 --> 00:10:08,127
only the graduation year cannot change the
interview call.

211
00:10:08,177 --> 00:10:10,260
Outside that range, the proof says
nothing.

212
00:10:10,610 --> 00:10:13,927
A detector's scores, grey for clean
inputs, red for attacked ones.

213
00:10:13,977 --> 00:10:17,907
A detection-based defense flags inputs
that look attacked and routes them to a

214
00:10:17,957 --> 00:10:19,131
fallback or a person.

215
00:10:19,181 --> 00:10:21,886
Here it catches seventy-four point five
percent of attacks.

216
00:10:21,936 --> 00:10:23,723
It does not fix the model.

217
00:10:23,773 --> 00:10:28,008
For example, a job portal flags résumés
that look doctored, such as ones packed

218
00:10:28,058 --> 00:10:31,069
with invisible characters, and sends them
to a human recruiter.

219
00:10:31,119 --> 00:10:32,600
The screener itself is unchanged.

220
00:10:32,950 --> 00:10:36,782
Input preprocessing changes the input
before the model sees it, by denoising,

221
00:10:36,832 --> 00:10:37,753
quantizing or smoothing.

222
00:10:37,803 --> 00:10:39,694
Here a filter averages neighbouring
values.

223
00:10:39,744 --> 00:10:43,900
With no defense, the FGSM push shifts the
score by six point four.

224
00:10:43,950 --> 00:10:47,782
With the filter, the alternating pushes
cancel and the shift is zero.

225
00:10:47,832 --> 00:10:51,988
For example, a portal turns every uploaded
résumé into plain visible text before

226
00:10:52,038 --> 00:10:55,546
screening, which strips out hidden
characters and keywords hidden in white

227
00:10:55,596 --> 00:10:55,870
text.

228
00:10:56,220 --> 00:11:00,838
An adaptive attack is built by someone who
knows the defense, and is designed to get

229
00:11:00,888 --> 00:11:01,421
past it.

230
00:11:01,471 --> 00:11:05,797
The new red bar pushes where the filter
does not erase, and the answer flips.

231
00:11:05,847 --> 00:11:09,298
Against the detector, the catch rate drops
to thirty-one point seven percent.

232
00:11:09,348 --> 00:11:13,091
For example, an applicant who knows the
portal strips hidden text writes the

233
00:11:13,141 --> 00:11:16,300
stuffed keywords as visible, ordinary-
looking phrases that the cleanup keeps.

234
00:11:16,650 --> 00:11:19,130
Two lines over the six months after
training.

235
00:11:19,180 --> 00:11:23,240
Distribution shift means the inputs a
deployed model meets drift away from its

236
00:11:23,290 --> 00:11:24,821
training inputs, with no attacker.

237
00:11:24,871 --> 00:11:28,932
In this simulated run accuracy, the red
line, falls to sixty-five percent while

238
00:11:28,982 --> 00:11:31,145
confidence does not fall, ending at
eighty-eight.

239
00:11:31,195 --> 00:11:35,256
For example, a screener trained on cover
letters written before AI writing tools

240
00:11:35,306 --> 00:11:37,469
spread now meets letters drafted with
them.

241
00:11:37,519 --> 00:11:38,418
Nobody attacks it.

242
00:11:38,468 --> 00:11:41,580
Its calls get worse while it stays just as
sure.

243
00:11:41,930 --> 00:11:45,431
Four pairs of sentences, each pair the
same meaning in different words.

244
00:11:45,481 --> 00:11:49,278
Semantic invariance says the answer should
not change when only the wording does.

245
00:11:49,328 --> 00:11:54,308
This toy rule adds up word scores, and
gives the same answer on only one of the

246
00:11:54,358 --> 00:11:54,900
four pairs.

247
00:11:54,950 --> 00:11:59,043
For example, one résumé says managed the
support team, another says was the support

248
00:11:59,093 --> 00:11:59,635
team's manager.

249
00:11:59,685 --> 00:12:02,890
A screener with semantic invariance gives
both the same fit score.

250
00:12:03,240 --> 00:12:06,128
One instruction, phrased five ways, for
two made-up models.

251
00:12:06,178 --> 00:12:09,719
Prompt sensitivity means prompts with the
same meaning get noticeably different

252
00:12:09,769 --> 00:12:10,046
answers.

253
00:12:10,096 --> 00:12:14,290
Model A's bars change a lot, a sensitivity
of zero point three eight.

254
00:12:14,340 --> 00:12:16,902
Model B's barely move, zero point zero
five.

255
00:12:16,952 --> 00:12:22,125
For example, a job seeker types to an AI
agent, tailor my résumé to this posting.

256
00:12:22,175 --> 00:12:25,716
Later the same person types, adapt my
résumé for this job.

257
00:12:25,766 --> 00:12:30,614
Same request, yet one answer trims a few
lines and the other rewrites the whole

258
00:12:30,664 --> 00:12:30,940
page.

259
00:12:31,290 --> 00:12:34,066
Three test results, with clean accuracy as
the outline.

260
00:12:34,116 --> 00:12:38,461
Adversarial testing means running inputs
built to fool the model, to find what it

261
00:12:38,511 --> 00:12:39,089
relies on.

262
00:12:39,139 --> 00:12:43,171
In this made-up example clean accuracy is
ninety-four percent, and every test lands

263
00:12:43,221 --> 00:12:43,799
below it.

264
00:12:43,849 --> 00:12:47,880
For example, before launch, a team feeds
its résumé screener doctored résumés, with

265
00:12:47,930 --> 00:12:52,276
stuffed keywords, hidden text and reworded
job titles, to see what it really relies

266
00:12:52,326 --> 00:12:52,590
on.

267
00:12:52,940 --> 00:12:57,191
A robustness profile says what the model
resists and what it does not, by attack,

268
00:12:57,241 --> 00:12:58,051
budget and shift.

269
00:12:58,101 --> 00:13:00,058
It is a table, not one number.

270
00:13:00,108 --> 00:13:03,498
Average these three results and you get
seventy-two percent, which hides the

271
00:13:03,548 --> 00:13:05,505
weakest one, the fifty-eight percent
transfer attack.

272
00:13:05,555 --> 00:13:09,519
For example, the maker of an interview-
prediction model reports a table, with a

273
00:13:09,569 --> 00:13:12,960
row each for ordinary résumés, keyword
stuffing at a stated budget, and

274
00:13:13,010 --> 00:13:14,680
applications from a newer hiring season.

275
00:13:15,030 --> 00:13:17,783
Six attack classes, each with a tick or a
cross.

276
00:13:17,833 --> 00:13:21,426
Residual risk disclosure is the written
list of known weaknesses that were not

277
00:13:21,476 --> 00:13:22,547
tested or not fixed.

278
00:13:22,597 --> 00:13:26,752
In this example four of the six were not
tested, and each one is named.

279
00:13:26,802 --> 00:13:30,675
For example, an agent that sends job
applications ships with a note saying it

280
00:13:30,725 --> 00:13:34,599
was never tested against prompt injection
hidden in job listings, and that this risk

281
00:13:34,649 --> 00:13:35,440
is not fixed.

282
00:13:35,790 --> 00:13:38,509
The share of inputs flagged each week in
production.

283
00:13:38,559 --> 00:13:42,201
A monitoring specification names the
signal being watched, and the value that

284
00:13:42,251 --> 00:13:43,739
triggers human review or rollback.

285
00:13:43,789 --> 00:13:46,816
Here review triggers at four percent,
crossed in week seven.

286
00:13:46,866 --> 00:13:48,969
Rollback, at eight percent, is never
reached.

287
00:13:49,019 --> 00:13:52,661
For example, a résumé screener's spec
watches the weekly share of résumés

288
00:13:52,711 --> 00:13:53,584
flagged as doctored.

289
00:13:53,634 --> 00:13:55,738
Past a set level, a recruiter reviews.

290
00:13:55,788 --> 00:13:59,430
Past a higher level, the team rolls back
to the earlier version.

291
00:13:59,780 --> 00:14:01,374
Three rungs for the panda case.

292
00:14:01,424 --> 00:14:04,661
Pearl's ladder sorts causal questions into
three levels, seeing, doing and imagining.

293
00:14:04,711 --> 00:14:08,222
Rung one is answered by watching the
model, rung two by an experiment.

294
00:14:08,272 --> 00:14:12,332
Rung three asks about a model that was
never built, so no data answers it.

295
00:14:12,382 --> 00:14:13,975
For example, take a hiring model.

296
00:14:14,025 --> 00:14:15,619
Seeing, whom does it rank first?

297
00:14:15,669 --> 00:14:18,359
Doing, what if we retrain it without the
school field?

298
00:14:18,409 --> 00:14:21,646
Imagining, would this rejected applicant
have been invited had it learned real

299
00:14:21,696 --> 00:14:21,920
skill?

300
00:14:22,270 --> 00:14:25,186
The same input, two models, before and
after the push.

301
00:14:25,236 --> 00:14:27,559
An intervention changes something and
watches what follows.

302
00:14:27,609 --> 00:14:31,119
A counterfactual asks about this one case,
had one thing been different.

303
00:14:31,169 --> 00:14:34,975
In this toy, a model that learned only
shape would still say panda.

304
00:14:35,025 --> 00:14:38,238
For example, an intervention drops the
employment-gap field for everyone and

305
00:14:38,288 --> 00:14:39,127
watches interview rates.

306
00:14:39,177 --> 00:14:42,094
A counterfactual asks whether this
particular applicant would have been

307
00:14:42,144 --> 00:14:44,170
invited had the screener ignored her gap.

308
00:14:44,520 --> 00:14:49,028
A chain from rules to goals to training to
this model, and a dashed copy.

309
00:14:49,078 --> 00:14:52,066
The governance question, also called the
governance counterfactual, asks whether

310
00:14:52,116 --> 00:14:55,713
the model would be different had the
institutions around it been different.

311
00:14:55,763 --> 00:14:57,536
Other rules, other goals, another model.

312
00:14:57,586 --> 00:15:01,790
For example, would a hiring model still
lean on polished keywords had the company

313
00:15:01,840 --> 00:15:05,740
that built it been judged on hires who
stayed, not on screening speed?

314
00:15:06,090 --> 00:15:07,647
Two pictures of one boundary.

315
00:15:07,697 --> 00:15:08,932
Left, an adversarial example.

316
00:15:08,982 --> 00:15:13,751
One point is pushed across, built by an
attacker as the worst case inside the

317
00:15:13,801 --> 00:15:14,073
budget.

318
00:15:14,123 --> 00:15:15,037
Right, distribution shift.

319
00:15:15,087 --> 00:15:17,607
Every point drifts, and no attacker is
involved.

320
00:15:17,657 --> 00:15:20,820
Ask whether someone built the change, or
the world moved.

321
00:15:21,170 --> 00:15:22,340
Left, an empirical defense.

322
00:15:22,390 --> 00:15:26,917
Six attacks were tried and it survived all
six, which says nothing about the next

323
00:15:26,967 --> 00:15:27,222
one.

324
00:15:27,272 --> 00:15:31,799
Right, a certified defense, a proof that
nothing gets in, up to a stated radius.

325
00:15:31,849 --> 00:15:34,240
Surviving the attacks tried is not a
proof.

326
00:15:34,590 --> 00:15:36,879
Clean accuracy is measured on ordinary
inputs.

327
00:15:36,929 --> 00:15:41,222
Robust accuracy is measured under a worst-
case push at a stated budget.

328
00:15:41,272 --> 00:15:45,899
In the simulated run the same model scores
one hundred percent clean and twenty-four

329
00:15:45,949 --> 00:15:47,235
point five percent robust.

330
00:15:47,285 --> 00:15:49,240
Ask which one you are shown.

331
00:15:49,590 --> 00:15:50,240
Left, detection.

332
00:15:50,290 --> 00:15:55,136
The detector flags a suspect input and
routes it to review, without changing it.

333
00:15:55,186 --> 00:15:55,836
Right, preprocessing.

334
00:15:55,886 --> 00:16:00,733
The jagged grey input comes out of the
filter as the flat red line.

335
00:16:00,783 --> 00:16:01,782
Detection sorts inputs.

336
00:16:01,832 --> 00:16:04,580
Preprocessing changes them before the
model sees them.

337
00:16:04,930 --> 00:16:08,197
A vague request, and the exact statement
that replaces it.

338
00:16:08,247 --> 00:16:11,514
Make it robust becomes, name the attack
and the budget.

339
00:16:11,564 --> 00:16:16,158
In this simulated run, under an L infinity
white-box attack at epsilon zero point

340
00:16:16,208 --> 00:16:18,148
two, twenty-five percent are still right.

341
00:16:18,198 --> 00:16:20,470
The line falls as the budget grows.

342
00:16:20,820 --> 00:16:23,156
The request is, the model is fragile.

343
00:16:23,206 --> 00:16:24,859
Fragile says nothing about why.

344
00:16:24,909 --> 00:16:26,904
The exact version names the proxy.

345
00:16:26,954 --> 00:16:30,994
Texture carries ninety-three percent of
the score and shape seven percent, the

346
00:16:31,044 --> 00:16:33,720
long red bar against the short dark one.

347
00:16:34,070 --> 00:16:36,600
The request is, harden it against attacks.

348
00:16:36,650 --> 00:16:38,812
Say how, and report both numbers.

349
00:16:38,862 --> 00:16:41,024
Train at epsilon zero point two.

350
00:16:41,074 --> 00:16:45,078
Robust accuracy rises from twenty-five to
seventy-five percent, and clean accuracy

351
00:16:45,128 --> 00:16:47,290
falls from one hundred to eighty-four.

352
00:16:47,640 --> 00:16:50,126
The request is, prove it cannot be fooled.

353
00:16:50,176 --> 00:16:52,662
No proof covers everything, so certify a
radius.

354
00:16:52,712 --> 00:16:57,733
With noise sigma of zero point one, the
red bar, the certified radius R is zero

355
00:16:57,783 --> 00:17:01,220
point two five for this one input, and
nothing beyond it.

356
00:17:01,570 --> 00:17:04,127
The request is, check it copes with
rewording.

357
00:17:04,177 --> 00:17:07,060
The exact version reports semantic
invariance as a count.

358
00:17:07,110 --> 00:17:11,297
The toy rule gives the same answer on one
of four reworded pairs.

359
00:17:11,347 --> 00:17:14,230
The crosses mark the pairs where the
answer changed.

360
00:17:14,580 --> 00:17:18,628
A checklist of six questions to ask before
you trust a robustness claim.

361
00:17:18,678 --> 00:17:20,520
The first is, robust against what.

362
00:17:20,570 --> 00:17:24,618
The attack class has to be named, such as
white-box, transfer or patch.

363
00:17:24,668 --> 00:17:27,140
Robust with nothing named is not a claim.

364
00:17:27,490 --> 00:17:28,413
Next, the budget.

365
00:17:28,463 --> 00:17:32,955
Ask for the perturbation budget, and for
the accuracy under attack reported next to

366
00:17:33,005 --> 00:17:33,928
the clean accuracy.

367
00:17:33,978 --> 00:17:38,470
A robust accuracy means nothing until you
know the budget it was measured at.

368
00:17:38,820 --> 00:17:39,634
Next, the proxy.

369
00:17:39,684 --> 00:17:44,239
Ask what proxy the model might lean on,
and which feature a person would use that

370
00:17:44,289 --> 00:17:45,391
it stands in for.

371
00:17:45,441 --> 00:17:49,709
A model that learned a proxy needs its
reliance inspected, not just a patch on

372
00:17:49,759 --> 00:17:50,860
the spot that broke.

373
00:17:51,210 --> 00:17:54,197
Then ask whether the defense is tested or
certified.

374
00:17:54,247 --> 00:17:56,896
An empirical defense held against the
attacks tried.

375
00:17:56,946 --> 00:18:00,271
A certified defense comes with a proof,
inside a radius.

376
00:18:00,321 --> 00:18:04,320
Either way, ask what sits outside that
radius, or outside its scope.

377
00:18:04,670 --> 00:18:09,043
Then ask which attacks and which shifts
were not tested, and whether that is

378
00:18:09,093 --> 00:18:09,675
written down.

379
00:18:09,725 --> 00:18:12,203
That written list is the residual risk
disclosure.

380
00:18:12,253 --> 00:18:16,310
Silence about an untested attack is not
evidence that the model resists it.

381
00:18:16,660 --> 00:18:21,287
Last, ask what is monitored in production,
and what triggers review or rollback.

382
00:18:21,337 --> 00:18:23,086
That is the monitoring specification.

383
00:18:23,136 --> 00:18:24,525
Then say it exactly.

384
00:18:24,575 --> 00:18:25,964
Not, it is robust.

385
00:18:26,014 --> 00:18:30,281
Say, for example, seventy-one percent
under an L infinity white-box attack at

386
00:18:30,331 --> 00:18:32,080
epsilon zero point zero three.

387
00:18:32,930 --> 00:18:33,488
Let's recap.

388
00:18:33,538 --> 00:18:37,139
A small push flips the answer because the
model learned a proxy.

389
00:18:37,189 --> 00:18:40,790
FGSM takes one step, and tiny pushes add
up across many inputs.

390
00:18:40,840 --> 00:18:44,136
White-box, black-box and transfer attacks
differ in what the attacker has.

391
00:18:44,186 --> 00:18:47,787
Every defense has a limit, and an adaptive
attack aims at it.

392
00:18:47,837 --> 00:18:50,525
Distribution shift and natural adversarial
inputs need no attacker.

393
00:18:50,575 --> 00:18:54,480
A robustness claim names the attack, the
budget and what was not tested.

394
00:18:54,830 --> 00:18:56,460
Quiz me on robustness terms.

395
00:18:56,510 --> 00:18:59,148
Give me one short case at a time.

396
00:18:59,198 --> 00:19:03,516
I must name the exact term and say why its
look-alike is wrong.

397
00:19:03,566 --> 00:19:06,204
Use adversarial example, distribution
shift, natural adversarial, white-box

398
00:19:06,254 --> 00:19:09,564
attack, transfer attack, adaptive attack,
certified defense, robust accuracy and

399
00:19:09,614 --> 00:19:10,572
residual risk disclosure.

400
00:19:10,622 --> 00:19:14,940
Hold back the answer until I reply, then
correct me in one sentence.

401
00:19:15,290 --> 00:19:16,731
Key Terms: Robustness.

402
00:19:16,781 --> 00:19:18,720
At Nik Bear Brown.
